ValleyMorph Tool — QGIS

This repository is an updated version of the ValleyMorph Tool, adapted for QGIS.

Based on:
- Original tool: https://github.com/turugban/ValleyMorphTool (turugban, 2014)
- ArcMap update: https://github.com/Phillip-Ruscica/ValleyMorphTool (Phillip Ruscica, 2023)

The full commit history of the original project is preserved here. All credit for the original method and code goes to the authors of the related article below.

QGIS plugin
-----------

The qgis_plugin/valleymorph_tool folder is a QGIS plugin that adds the ValleyMorph Tool to the Processing Toolbox (and to the Raster menu). It is a port of the ArcGIS toolbox script (script/arctoolbox/river_transect.py): the same inputs, the same steps in the same order, and the same nine outputs with the same names, fields and summary tables. It needs no ArcGIS licence and no extra Python packages (it uses numpy and scipy, which ship with QGIS). Because it is a Processing algorithm it also works in the Graphical Modeler, in batch mode and from PyQGIS (processing.run("valleymorph:valleymorph", {...})).

Install
1. Zip the valleymorph_tool folder (the zip must contain the folder itself), or copy the folder into your QGIS plugins directory (Settings > User Profiles > Open Active Profile Folder, then python/plugins).
2. In QGIS: Plugins > Manage and Install Plugins > Install from ZIP (or enable "ValleyMorph Tool" on the Installed tab if you copied the folder).
3. The tool appears in the Processing Toolbox under "ValleyMorph Tool" and in Raster > ValleyMorph Tool.

Inputs (same as the ArcGIS tool)
- Watershed DEM. Should extend at least two cells beyond the watersheds; otherwise elevations on the watershed edge are NoData (-9999).
- Watershed boundary (polygons, same coordinate system as the DEM; a projected system in meters is expected).
- Watershed ID: integer field, or text convertible to integer. Leave empty to use the feature ID (the original used a shapefile's FID).
- Longaxis interval in meters (default 50).
- Midline and river profile interval (MRPI) in meters (default 500).
- Number of MRPI (0 = no limit).
- Adjust for edge effects (shrinks each watershed by two cells).
- Fill sinks (not in the original tool, off by default). Turn it on when the DEM has not been hydrologically filled and watersheds are skipped with "riverline_connected too short", which happens when the highest flow accumulation falls in a pit inside the watershed. It only changes flow routing (lowpoint and river); elevations and slopes still come from the DEM as given.
- Output workspace: a GeoPackage (.gpkg), or an ESRI File Geodatabase (.gdb) like the original.

Outputs (same as the ArcGIS tool): rt_rivercrosslines, rt_midcrosslines, rt_watershedmidline, rt_longest_riverline, rt_valleyfloor, rt_elevationpoints, rt_midcrosslinestable, rt_elevationpointstable, rt_valleyfloorwidthtable.

Agreement with the ArcGIS version
qgis_plugin/tests/compare_with_arcgis.py runs the plugin on the sample data in test/ and compares it with the ArcGIS output in test/output.gdb. On that data the lowpoints, farpoints, watershed midlines and midline crosslines match ArcGIS (88% of the midline summary table values agree to within 1 m, and the rest follow from the river differences below). The main river follows the same course but can differ by a few cells where it crosses large flat areas, because ArcGIS 10.1 does not document how its Flow Direction tool routes flow across flats or breaks ties, and its raster-to-polygon "simplify" option is likewise undocumented. Those small differences carry through to the river crosslines, the valley floor (within 3% of the ArcGIS area) and the valley floor widths and elevations. A few other implementation details:
- ArcGIS's undocumented raster behaviour was matched to the reference output where possible: ExtractByMask grid alignment, single-cell sink filling, flow off NoData edges, Strahler order on the flow accumulation raster (0 = background), and StreamToFeature line ends half a cell beyond source and outlet cells.
- Crosslines are geodesic (as in BearingDistanceToLine GEODESIC) but stored as straight two-point lines.
- When watersheds produce different summary-table columns, the combined table has all columns (ArcGIS's Append dropped columns missing from the first watershed).
- The extra options in VMT_100_Package (FID range, midline smoothing, per-range output folders and the merger tool) are not part of the port.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

ValleyMorph Tool

Related article: Daxberger, H., Dalumpines, R., Scott, D.M., Riller, U. (in press). The ValleyMorph Tool: An Automated Extraction Tool for Transverse Topographic Symmetry (T-) Factor and Valley Width to Valley Height (Vf) Ratio. Computers & Geosciences, available online 7 June 2014. DOI: 10.1016/j.cageo.2014.05.015

The ValleyMorph tool creates crosslines (perpendicular to) or profile lines of watershed midline. For each predefined interval, the tool measures distances from the midline to the river, and from the river to the watershed boundary. The tool extracts elevation values for points defined by the intersection of the crosslines, watershed boundary, and the extent of valley floor. Aside from the elevation values, the tool also measures the width of the valley floor for each interval. Most of the results are summarized in tables, with feature classes included for validation and other purposes. The tool's outputs, which consist of six file geodatabase feature classes and three tables, are stored in a user-defined file geodatabase. The output filenames can be identified with "rt_" prefix.

This repository consists of two main folders: script and test. The script folder contains the two versions of the tool, which can be used as a standalone (idle subfolder) or can be added in ArcMap as a tool (arctoolbox subfolder). Both versions were tested in ArcGIS 10.1, with the standalone version to be more stable. The test folder contains a sample watershed and corresponding DEM that can be used to explore the ValleyMorph's functionality. Also, the test folder includes an output file geodatabase that contains the tool's outputs using the default settings. Note that some elevation points extracted have no data values due to limited pixels along the watershed boundaries (this can be avoided by setting the option for Adjust for Edge Effects to True). For more information about the tool (including information on further processing of the data generated by the tool), see the related article cited above.

As a guide, please use the toolbox version of the tool because it provides a simple GUI and basic information on the tool's inputs. For detailed information on how to add the toolbox version to ArcMap, please consult ArcGIS help on using ArcToolbox window. Once familiar with the inputs in the toolbox version, one may use the standalone version (tested in Python's IDLE) to process huge datasets. For the standalone version, the user needs to change the following input parameters at the bottom of the Python file (river_transect_standalone.py):

# Set input parameters.
workspace = r"C:\Users\Ron\Documents\GitHub\ValleyMorphTool\test\output.gdb"  # output workspace (file geodatabase)
in_raster = r"C:\Users\Ron\Documents\GitHub\ValleyMorphTool\test\wsheddem"  # watershed DEM (raster layer)
watershedbound = r"C:\Users\Ron\Documents\GitHub\ValleyMorphTool\test\wshedbound.shp"  # watershed boundaries (feature layer)
watershedfid = "FID"  # watershed ID (fieldname)
interval = 50  # longaxis interval in meters (float)
interval_profile = 500  # midline and river profile interval in meters (float)
count_intervalpr = None  # number of midline and river profile interval (integer)
adjust_edge = "False"  # adjustment for edge effects (boolean string)

For detailed information on how to edit and run the standalone version, please consult www.python.org on IDLE documentation.